  "title": "TypeScript Conditional Types & infer Explained (2026)",
  "description": "Conditional types are where TypeScript stops feeling like a typing tool and starts feeling like a programming language of its own. In this video you'll learn how to write type-level if statements using the extends keyword, and how the powerful infer keyword lets you extract types from inside other types. If you've ever wondered how libraries like React, Zod, and tRPC produce those magical auto-completing types, conditional types are the secret engine under the hood.\n\nWe start with the basic extends ? : syntax and build up to distributive conditional types, nested conditions, and real-world utility types like ReturnType, Awaited, and Parameters — many of which you can now write yourself from scratch. Then we go deep on infer: pulling out array element types, function return types, promise payloads, and tuple heads and tails. Along the way we recreate built-in utilities, handle edge cases like never and union distribution, and look at when conditional types help versus when they over-complicate your code.\n\nIf this video helps the type system finally click, hit subscribe and check out the full TypeScript Mastery playlist. Drop a comment with the trickiest type you've ever had to write — we read every one and feature the best questions in future videos. Let's get into it.\n\n─────────────────────────────\n📚 CHAPTERS\n\n00:00 Introduction\n00:00 What problem conditional types solve\n00:45 The basic extends syntax\n01:40 Reading the resolved result\n02:30 A practical first example\n03:25 Nesting conditional types\n04:25 Distributive conditional types\n05:30 Stopping distribution\n06:25 The never edge case\n07:20 Introducing the infer keyword\n08:20 How infer pattern matching works\n09:15 Inferring function return types\n10:15 Inferring function parameters\n11:05 Unwrapping Promises with infer\n12:05 Inferring from tuples — head and tail\n13:05 Multiple infer in one pattern\n14:00 Rebuilding NonNullable\n14:50 Building Exclude and Extract\n15:45 A real-world API example\n16:50 Inferring string literal types\n17:50 Testing your types compile\n18:40 When NOT to use conditional types\n19:35 Quick mental model recap\n20:25 Recreate a built-in challenge\n\n─────────────────────────────\n🔗 LINKS\n📺 Full Course Playlist: https://www.youtube.com/@SukridLearnHub\n🌐 Website: https://www.sukrid.com/learnhub\n\n─────────────────────────────\n⚡ SUKRID LEARNHUB\nFree technology education — AI, TypeScript, React, Next.js, Node.js, Golang, and Engineering.\nNew videos regularly. All content free, forever.\n\n#typescript #typescript conditional types #infer keyword typescript #typescript tutorial #advanced typescript",
